<h1>Maze application</h1>
<p>For tutorial purposes, we developed an application to act as a system under test - software under test, in
  this case - called <strong>Maze</strong>, which we'll use as the target of the tests we develop throughout this
  tutorial.</p>

<h2 id="download">Download</h2>
<p>We'll need all three versions later on in this tutorial. Create a <code>bin</code> folder with a subfolder per
  version, and download the executable matching your operating system into its version's subfolder:</p>
<ul>
  <li><a class="text-accent" href="https://github.com/GMCoreAI/orcraft-maze-app/releases/tag/maze-v1.0.0">v1.0.0</a></li>
  <li><a class="text-accent" href="https://github.com/GMCoreAI/orcraft-maze-app/releases/tag/maze-v2.0.0">v2.0.0</a></li>
  <li><a class="text-accent" href="https://github.com/GMCoreAI/orcraft-maze-app/releases/tag/maze-v3.0.0">v3.0.0</a></li>
</ul>
<p>Each release provides a Windows, Linux and macOS executable, so the result looks like this (keep only the one
  matching your operating system):</p>
{{ codeblock.block('bin/
  v1.0.0/
    maze-v1.0.0-win.exe
    maze-v1.0.0-linux
    maze-v1.0.0-macos
  v2.0.0/
    maze-v2.0.0-win.exe
    maze-v2.0.0-linux
    maze-v2.0.0-macos
  v3.0.0/
    maze-v3.0.0-win.exe
    maze-v3.0.0-linux
    maze-v3.0.0-macos', 'text') }}
<p class="text-danger">This repository is currently private; the links above will be updated once it is public.</p>

<h2 id="usage">Usage</h2>
<p>Run the executable with <code>--help</code> to see its available commands and options:</p>
{% ansiOutput "maze-help.ansi" %}

<h2 id="run-examples">Run examples</h2>

<h3 id="generating-a-maze">Generating a maze</h3>
<p>Generate a 20×8 maze with a fixed seed, so it comes out identical on every run:</p>
{{ cmd.copyCommand("maze generate --width 20 --height 8 --seed 42") }}
{% ansiOutput "maze-generate.txt" %}
<p>The maze is printed as ASCII art, with <code>S</code> marking the start cell and <code>E</code> the end cell.</p>

<h3 id="solving-a-maze">Solving a maze</h3>
<p>Solve that same maze with the A* algorithm:</p>
{{ cmd.copyCommand("maze solve --width 20 --height 8 --seed 42 --algorithm astar") }}
{% ansiOutput "maze-solve.txt" %}
<p>The solved maze shows the path from start to end as arrows, followed by the number of steps and the algorithm
  that was used.</p>

<h2 id="generating-maze-save-files">Generating maze save files</h2>
<p>Later in this tutorial we'll need saved mazes to feed into tests as file parameters. The <code>save</code>
  command generates a maze and writes it to a JSON file:</p>
{{ cmd.copyCommand("maze save --width 20 --height 10 --seed 7 --output maze.json") }}
{% ansiOutput "maze-save.txt" %}
<p>Let's create three of them, each with different dimensions and a fixed seed, so everyone following this
  tutorial ends up with the exact same mazes:</p>
{{ cmd.copyCommand("maze save --width 20 --height 10 --seed 7 --output maze.json") }}
{{ cmd.copyCommand("maze save --width 30 --height 15 --seed 42 --output maze2.json") }}
{{ cmd.copyCommand("maze save --width 40 --height 20 --seed 99 --output maze3.json") }}
<p>Run these from the <code>v3.0.0</code> folder, so the three files sit next to the executable:</p>
{{ codeblock.block('bin/
  v3.0.0/
    maze-v3.0.0-win.exe
    maze.json
    maze2.json
    maze3.json', 'text') }}
<p>A saved maze can be printed again with <code>load</code>, or solved with <code>solve --file</code>, without
  regenerating it:</p>
{{ cmd.copyCommand("maze load --file maze.json") }}
{{ cmd.copyCommand("maze solve --file maze.json --algorithm dfs") }}
